<code>assert index >= 0 && index < \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()) - 1; (this.get(i) == null ? this.get(i + 1) == null : this.get(i + 1).equals(\old(this.get(i))));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert index >= 0 && index < \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()) - 1; (this.get(i) == null ? this.get(i + 1) == null : this.get(i + 1).equals(\old(this.get(i))));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i) == null && \old(this.get(i+1)) == null) || this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i+1)));</code>

However, there is a small issue in the above assertion. The loop condition and the comparison need to be adjusted to correctly reflect the shift in elements after insertion. Here's the corrected version:

<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index <= i && i < \old(this.size()); (this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i+1))));</code>

But since the element at the `index` position is replaced by the new `element`, the correct assertion should account for the shift and the new element at the `index` position. Here is the revised assertion:

<code>assert index >= 0 && index <= \old(this.size()) => \forall int i; index < i && i < \old(this.size()); this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i-1)));</code>

This ensures that each element from `index+1` to the old size minus one is shifted to the next position. The element at `index` is the newly added element, so it doesn't need to be compared in the loop.
